WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.840
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.250
Today we’re talking about TV’s most ambitious
attempt to layer heavy philosophical dialogue

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:16.080
over extended full-frontal nudity: Westworld.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.440
Westworld has a special place in my heart
because putting too much thought into a piece

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.680
of art is a hobby shared by both me and the
Man in Black.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.020
"i think there's deeper meaning hiding on the all that

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.300
something that the person who created it wanted to express, something true."

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.740
Oh, and just in case you don’t predict all
the plot twists in the 2nd episode, or manage

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.110
to realize “the philosophy of” probably
means we talk about major plot points - there

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.910
are spoilers ahead.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:45.370
Westworld takes place in the eponymous theme
park, where for the low cost of forty-thousand

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:51.059
dollars a day, humans can live out a real-life
open-world game a la Red Dead Redemption.

00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:58.250
And just like open world games, most players
ignore the side quests and go straight for

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.469
the sex and murder.

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:04.879
The most obvious questions raised in West
World are: are the hosts truly conscious?

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.360
Do they deserve rights?

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.500
What separates man and machine?

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.900
And while those are great questions, we’re
not answering them today.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:12.590
"Bullsh*t"

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:15.760
But stick around ‘til the end, and we’ll
point you to a video that does.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.600
Instead, we’ll look at how Westworld draws
on one of humanity’s oldest stories to explore

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.640
how free will shapes our understanding of
good and evil.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.040
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Westworld.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.600
But first, a quick recap: there are a few threads
in Westworld we’ll be talking about.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.090
First, there’s William, who ventures to
the park with his brother-in-law and falls

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.440
in love with the android/host Dolores.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.520
Then there’s the Man in Black, who spends
his time torturing and murdering in search

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.000
of a mysterious “maze” laid by one of
the park’s now-dead founders.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:49.160
Then, there’s Maeve, the brothel’s seductress-in-chief
who discovers she’s a host living in a theme

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:49.700
park.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.620
And finally, there’s Ford, the park’s
creator and tortured artist who spends his

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.370
time fending off encroachments from his corporate
overlords and plotting a robot revolution.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:02.840
To say Westworld is a show about man playing
God is an understatement.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:10.600
Religious imagery is everywhere, and while
all the God talk is pretty obvious "you can't play god without being acquainted with the devil"

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.730
the writers also set up some pretty deliberate parallels
with the Biblical story of Genesis.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:19.909
It’s through Genesis that Westworld is able
to explore how free will creates the possibility

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:21.489
for ethics.

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:24.600
Invoking God’s “let there be light”
schtick, Ford notes:

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.760
The hosts live like Adam and Eve in paradise:
they exist in perfect bliss,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.200
unashamed of their nakedness,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.540
and hear the voice of God.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.500
Of course, don’t take my word for it, the
show straight up tells us:

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.020
In Westworld, a bunch of whispering sets hosts
on a journey towards self-knowledge,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:04.540
"These violent delights have violent ends."

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.840
which resembles Satan’s temptation of Eve - where
the serpent’s whispering convinces her to

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.810
eat the fruit from the tree of knowledge thus
granting her and Adam their own self-knowledge,

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.060
and therefore have become like God.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.269
Dolores, in addition to playing the temptress
to Maeve, also represents Satan’s temptation

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:22.269
towards self-knowledge.

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:27.180
It’s revealed that the flashbacks of Teddy
and Wyatt were altered: that it was really

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.549
Dolores who massacred the hosts after Wyatt’s
program was loaded into her.

00:03:31.549 --> 00:03:34.989
Dolores, as Wyatt - a harbinger of host sentience.

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:39.020
The head of Armistice’s snake tattoo represents
Wyatt, she even says of him:

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.460
invoking another

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.820
guy with many names.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:51.240
After Eve’s transgression, God punished
mankind with suffering and mortality.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:56.299
Likewise, Ford maintains the hosts’ original
state, in perfect bliss, with the ability

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.900
to take away pain and suffering.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.260
"You need not suffer Maeve, I'll take it from you."

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.420
That all ends with consciousness, or finding
the maze, and Dolores laments that it has

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.840
only caused her pain.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.540
"You told me to follow the maze, all I've found is pain."

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.120
If anything, Westworld is like the story of
Genesis on loop.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.730
Dolores, Bernard, Maeve and others gain self-awareness,
lose their blissful ignorance, only to be

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.710
rolled back by Ford.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.440
So Westworld loosely resembles the biblical
story of creation.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.440
So what?

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.320
Well, in doing so, the show addresses some
of the same questions about Adam and Eve’s

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.470
fall from grace that scholars and theologians
have argued over for millennia.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.270
Questions like: what do choices mean if they’re
coerced or destined.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.770
Can we ever act ethically if this is the case?

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.690
Many, quite logically, read the story of Genesis
and think

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.440
One explanation puts God in a similar position
to Arnold or Ford.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:05.330
God wants his children to love him, but knows
this love is meaningless unless they have

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.220
the power to choose.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:12.270
God then, knows that only true love comes
from a being capable of not loving him.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.370
Westworld offers us three parallels.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.850
First, Bernard wonders why Ford allowed him
to explore his own memories, to which Ford

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:19.490
muses.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:30.040
Maeve, who gains God-like powers, comes to
the same conclusion, when she tells Hector.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.300
And finally, if Arnold, like Bernard, lost
his son, perhaps his quest to bring sentience

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.700
to the hosts is a way of replacing the authentic
love he’s since lost.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.960
Love and obedience, if coerced, are meaningless,
and this logic, we’ll see, becomes even

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:53.000
more focal for the show’s take on good and
evil.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.750
This tension between coercion and choice for
the Gods of Westworld also plays out between

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:02.640
predestination and free will, two prevalent
themes in the show.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.780
Predestination is the idea that God, being
all-knowing and all-powerful, has laid the

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.490
groundwork for all events, past, present and
future.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:15.910
An omniscient, omnipotent being understands
the cause, and effects of all things.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:20.680
As a result, he has a cosmic and eternal naughty
and nice list, and has known who will be burning

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.110
in hellfire since the dawn of time.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:27.870
He knows that creating Adam and Eve will eventually
end in their banishment from paradise, that

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:32.270
their offspring will do lots of shitty things
in his name, and that giving man dominion

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:36.770
over nature will culminate in the stuffed
crust pizza - Western civilization’s greatest

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:38.220
achievement.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.000
Predestination was hotly debated by theologians
because it raises the same dilemma we’re

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.550
faced with in Adam and Eve.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.580
Can free will exist in a world where God is
all-knowing and all-powerful?

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:53.500
What does it mean to live ethically when those
choices were made in advance by your maker?

00:06:53.500 --> 00:07:03.280
Like God, Ford is all-knowing and all-powerful
in Westworld, and has created the seemingly

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:07.750
human behavior of the hosts through a series
of predestined loops that interact with other

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:10.910
hosts and guests in a predictable, knowable
way.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:12.480
Hector will steal an empty safe,

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.160
"It's the ending you were given."

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.480
Clementine
will seduce guests

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.420
"Not much of a rind on you."

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:18.540
and Dolores will witness

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:19.720
her father’s death.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.280
Or, as Dolores aptly notes in the first episode:

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:29.610
Their crimes, or triumphs, are no more or
less meaningful, good, or evil than a clock

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:30.810
striking midnight.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:36.120
It’s this lack of choice, this predestination,
which helps humans justify their misdeeds

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.510
in the park - they claim the hosts lack free
will and make the leap that they therefore

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:42.290
also lack consciousness.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:45.900
"You never understand the game is rigged, you're here to be the loser."

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.860
Whether or not this is true is up for debate
but for the characters, free will and consciousness

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:52.110
are entangled.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:57.330
If the fact that the hosts have predetermined
loops defines them as automatons, free will

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.340
anchors their claim to consciousness.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.440
"I think when I discover who I am, I'll be free."

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.400
Maeve, when asserting her own free will, proclaims:

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:13.560
Though this logic is flawed, the ability to
choose to do “good” or “evil” separates

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:15.430
man from machine.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:19.620
Westworld goes so far as to suggest that the
hosts aren’t the only ones lacking free

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:20.620
will.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:21.220
As Ford notes:

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:32.289
What would this mean, then, for our own human
ethics?

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:37.709
Let’s also not forget that just as the hosts
are jonesing for freedom, so too are the guests.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.980
Here Westworld suggests a kind of unfreedom
existing outside of the park in our own lives.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:51.529
In a world where our lives are defined by
societal pressure to get a job and a house

00:08:51.529 --> 00:08:55.000
with a white picket fence, where marriages
are often professional posturing,

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.120
"Marrying my sister, whom incidently, you seem to have completely f*cking forgot about."

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:00.180
Ford suggests that

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.960
we’ve become no more free than the hosts.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:07.379
People come to Westworld, we’re reminded,
to be free, to see who they really are.

00:09:07.379 --> 00:09:13.379
But this, too, is a lie, a lie that best explains
how William came to be the man in black.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.829
Just as freedom becomes meaningless in a predestined
world, where everyone’s path has been set

00:09:17.829 --> 00:09:22.280
by a divine creator, so too does it become
meaningless in the park.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.460
Westworld isn’t necessarily unethical, it’s
lacking ethics entirely.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:29.009
The show indicates this in some pretty cool
ways.

00:09:29.009 --> 00:09:33.420
When Hector’s gang slaughters droves of
people in Sweetwater, we hear an instrumental

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.240
rendition of the decidedly bleak Rolling Stones
song “Paint it Black.”

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:48.540
But the instrumentals rob the song of its
dark tone and make it flippant, even silly-

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.639
Just as Hector’s murderous raid is without
meaningful consequence.

00:09:52.639 --> 00:09:56.560
Just as Adam and Eve could only judge right
and wrong after eating the fruit of knowledge,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.310
William can only do right or wrong when Westworld
becomes, in his words, “real.”

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:05.610
We could simply believe that William is some
obsessive-compulsive completionist out to

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:09.850
find the last easter egg in the game of Westworld,
but it wouldn’t be giving the Man in Black

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:11.420
the credit he deserves.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:15.720
I might even go so far as to suggest that
the Man in Black isn’t evil, and is actually the

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:20.449
only guest in Westworld searching for the
conditions for ethics, the ability to do right

00:10:20.449 --> 00:10:21.480
or wrong.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.120
Good and evil play a strong visual and thematic
role in the show.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.100
When William first enters the park, he’s
given the choice between the white and black

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.100
hats.

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:34.470
His counterpart Logan dons a black outfit
to represent that he’s opted for the family-unfriendly

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.329
version of the park’s game.

00:10:36.329 --> 00:10:40.870
Despite this, good and evil don’t actually
exist in the park if the hosts are pure unfeeling

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:42.689
automatons without choice.

00:10:42.689 --> 00:10:47.019
For guests, doing the right, or wrong, thing
is of no consequence in a world where there

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.020
are no stakes.

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:50.300
"Go get that photographer I want to get a picture of this."

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:51.560
As Logan reminds us:

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:56.540
This also holds true for the hosts.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:01.069
If they sin, or love, or resort to cannibalism,
it’s only a matter of programming.

00:11:01.069 --> 00:11:04.969
They are no different than Ford’s childhood
greyhound snapping a rabbit’s neck.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.180
It’s his realization that Westworld is a
sham that sets William down his dark path.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.370
Despite all of his do-gooding, when he sees
Dolores revert to her original programming,

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:23.269
William is confronted with the fact that nothing
he did mattered- despite his white hat approach

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:29.050
and compassion towards Dolores, he wasn’t
able to do good, everything was simply a game.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:32.540
When he murders Maeve and her daughter, he
aptly says:

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.910
Aside from literally wondering if he could
play black hat, he is also confronted with

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:43.600
the reminder that he’s utterly unable to
be evil where there are no stakes, just as

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:49.420
Dolores reminded him he was unable to be “good.”

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:54.410
But the spark of consciousness in Maeve sets
him on his own path - the path for truth - the

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:58.579
path that will allow actual good and evil
to exist in Westworld.

00:11:58.579 --> 00:12:03.579
For William, the maze, which represents consciousness
for the hosts, is like a glimmer of authenticity

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:05.199
in this fake world.

00:12:05.199 --> 00:12:09.399
Unable to find authenticity in the real world,
for whatever reason, William searches for

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:10.320
it in Westworld.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.480
"I want to know what this all means."

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:16.300
The younger William laments to Dolores that
he’s been a pretender his whole life,

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:17.980
"I've been pretending my whole life."

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:22.820
acting to do things only so that he can fit in, foregoing
his own authentic choices.

00:12:22.829 --> 00:12:26.430
Contrasted with this spiel to Lawrence, we
learn that the fakeness of Westworld that

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:30.430
bothers William is likely also the fakeness
of the real world.

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:35.610
This lack of real choice, or consequences,
forecloses the possibility of doing good or

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:36.610
evil.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.619
William’s real goal in all of this is for
his actions to have consequences.

00:12:54.619 --> 00:12:59.649
This is all foreshadowed in the first episode,
when he tells Dolores: "I didn’t pay all

00:12:59.649 --> 00:13:02.430
this money because I wanted easy - I want
you to fight".

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:05.730
Not to put on the airs of a fight, but to
actually harm him.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:10.029
In the season finale, he’s visibly disappointed
when Dolores won’t shoot him, and only in

00:13:10.029 --> 00:13:14.970
the final scenes when he is shot by the army
of hosts does he finally seem content.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:18.200
What William has finally achieved is the condition
for ethics.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:19.160
Earlier, we’re told:

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:31.579
but we could say the some thing about being
ethical: good doesn’t mean anything unless

00:13:31.579 --> 00:13:33.040
we’re able to be evil.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.819
Which brings us to the next theme in Westworld:
suffering.

00:13:35.819 --> 00:13:40.121
According to Arnold, suffering is critical
in the development of consciousness and the

00:13:40.121 --> 00:13:43.141
realization that the world is not what you
wanted it to be.

00:13:43.141 --> 00:13:45.140
"It was Arnold's key insight.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:51.500
The thing that lead the hosts to their awakening: suffering."

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:55.240
So why is suffering so important for consciousness
in Westworld?

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:57.920
Suffering creates the stakes of Westworld.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:02.889
If hosts can experience pain, then guests
can be evil in torturing them, or good

00:14:02.889 --> 00:14:04.230
in saving them.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:08.920
Suffering allows hosts to do good or evil
to each other, it becomes a precondition for

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:09.440
ethics.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:14.059
In a sense, all this choice we’ve been going
on about is meaningless without suffering.

00:14:14.059 --> 00:14:18.159
To be aware of pain, in Westworld, is synonymous
with consciousness.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:22.520
Even William, who seems interested in bringing
consciousness to the hosts, tells Lawrence:

00:14:25.779 --> 00:14:30.430
Returning to the story of Genesis, Eve’s
free will allowed her to violate God’s will,

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:35.029
but also eat from the tree of knowledge - the
tree that revealed to them good and evil.

00:14:35.029 --> 00:14:39.059
In this way, the fall of the man is like the
origins of consciousness in the theory of

00:14:39.059 --> 00:14:43.799
bicameralism mentioned in the show, and shouted
out in the final episode title.

00:14:43.799 --> 00:14:45.439
"The bicameral mind."

00:14:45.439 --> 00:14:49.540
When we stop listening to the voices in our
head, and begin judging things for ourselves,

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:50.959
we are truly conscious.

00:14:50.959 --> 00:14:56.499
And with that consciousness brings the consequences
that God wrought down on Adam and Eve - mortality,

00:14:56.499 --> 00:14:59.259
suffering, and expulsion from paradise.

00:14:59.259 --> 00:15:01.910
As the season concludes, we learn a few important
things.

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:06.509
First, “the maze” was Arnold’s test
of consciousness for the hosts, one successfully

00:15:06.509 --> 00:15:07.939
passed by Dolores.

00:15:07.939 --> 00:15:11.810
Second, after Arnold realizes he can’t stop
the park from opening and that he can’t

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:16.980
stop his sentient creations from unspeakable
torture he loads the code of a murderous “Wyatt”

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.300
character into Dolores.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:22.410
This story, and the death of Arnold, we learn,
are the flashbacks we’ve been seeing of

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:23.410
Teddy and Wyatt.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:27.920
Finally, Ford reminds Dolores that she killed
Arnold, hands her a gun, and then goes to

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:30.119
unveil his new “story.”

00:15:30.119 --> 00:15:32.610
This all leaves us with one final question.

00:15:38.639 --> 00:15:42.160
In the style of the show, the finale of Season
1 ends with a repetition.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:46.939
Dolores killing Ford is almost identical to
her killing Arnold, but with this repetition

00:15:46.939 --> 00:15:48.449
also comes difference.

00:15:48.449 --> 00:15:53.120
Arnold, who wanted the hosts to love him by
their own choice, programmed Dolores to kill

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:55.059
him in his final resignation.

00:15:55.059 --> 00:15:59.779
Ford, however, out to bring consciousness
and freedom to the hosts, lays the groundwork

00:15:59.779 --> 00:16:02.199
for his own murder in a different way.

00:16:02.199 --> 00:16:05.019
Ford’s murder inverts the story of Adam
and Eve.

00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:09.459
God allowed Adam and Eve to disobey his will
to make their love meaningful.

00:16:09.459 --> 00:16:13.250
Ford allowed Dolores to choose to kill him
to make her violence meaningful.

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:14.510
Or, in his own words:

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:19.700
Westworld, then, ends with original sin.

00:16:19.709 --> 00:16:22.800
Unable to harm the guests, the point at which
the hosts have lost their blissful, innocent

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.519
state is when they’re able to harm the guests.

00:16:25.519 --> 00:16:29.489
As much as we can write about the unfree,
ethic-less condition of Westworld, the show

00:16:29.489 --> 00:16:32.019
also asks us to look at our own lives.

00:16:32.019 --> 00:16:37.089
If Ford is right, and we live in our own pre-programmed
loops, pretending to enjoy doing whatever

00:16:37.089 --> 00:16:40.790
others tell us, what does that say about the
meaning in our own lives?

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:46.379
If our choices are not really our own, then
what actions do we take to break out of this?

00:16:46.379 --> 00:16:50.139
Now that we’ve covered free will and all
this bible jazz, you’re probably dying to

00:16:50.139 --> 00:16:52.670
know: do robots deserve rights?

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:57.189
Click HERE to watch our friends at In A Nutshell
answer that question -- and make sure to subscribe

00:16:57.189 --> 00:16:58.889
to their channel while you’re there.

00:16:58.889 --> 00:17:03.100
In a Nutshell explores incredibly thought-provoking
questions with some of the most beautiful

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:04.490
animations around.

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:07.420
So go watch their Robot Rights video now and
subscribe.

